</script>

<Doc>
    <Code code={`abide check
abide check src/billing src/ui`} />

    <Section title="The errors it finds that `tsc` cannot">
        <p>Every one of these is caught before the code runs, which is why the ergonomic spelling
        does not have to be given up to make the strict one safe:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a read spelled <code>foo()</code> with an argument</td><td><code>foo</code> is not a function you call</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>props()</code> from a <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> block</td><td>there is no instance to read props of</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a prop passed to a component that never calls <code>props()</code></td><td>it accepts none</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a <code>Failed</code> built and discarded</td><td>the refusal did not happen</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a <code>reactive</code> handed to two handlers</td><td>names both</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a nested object argument on a <code>GET</code> or <code>DELETE</code></td><td>names the method, and says a <code>POST</code> is the fix</td></tr>
                <tr><td>two files answering one address</td><td>names both files</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a stylesheet or a helper directly beside the pages</td><td>names the file, and the <code>lib</code> to move it into</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a handler file exporting anything but its handler</td><td>names the export, and where it belongs instead</td></tr>
                <tr><td>an <code>export</code> in a <code>.abide</code></td><td>the component is the file's one export, and a handler another file calls is a handler file</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The last six are build errors rather than type errors, and they are here for the same
        reason: each is a mistake that otherwise fails at the first request, or worse, quietly
        succeeds.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/abide-files')}><code>.abide</code> files</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The line number is the point">
        <p>A framework that compiles templates to code and hands the errors to <code>tsc</code>
        reports on the code it generated. That is a position in a file you did not write, about a
        construct you did not spell, and mapping it back is a job the reader does every time.</p>

        <p><code>abide check</code> reports where you typed it. That is the whole of the feature and
        it is worth a command of its own.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`abide lsp` is the same check, in the editor">
        <Code code={`abide lsp`} />

        <p>The <code>.abide</code> language server, over stdio. It is the same analysis <code>abide
        check</code> runs, so the squiggle in your editor and the failure in CI are the same finding
        rather than two implementations that agree most of the time.</p>

        <p>A hover, a jump to a definition and the members offered after a <code>.</code> are
        answered from that same program, so <code>config().</code> offers the fields your
        <code>onConfig</code> defaults.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/cli')}>CLI</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A handler another file calls is a `.ts`">
        <p>A <code>.abide</code> exports its component and nothing else, so a handler a second file
        calls is a handler file — a <code>.ts</code> — and <code>tsc</code> reads it as it reads any
        other. A <code>.ts</code> importing it is typed with nothing generated in between and
        nothing to keep current.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`abide routes` is the table the build will serve">
        <Code code={`abide routes
abide routes --json`} />

        <p>Every address the app answers, the method and the file behind each, and the file filing
        each hook. It is the build's own reading of the tree, so an address you meant to add and
        did not is missing here before it is missing in production.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/find-the-url-a-handler-answers-on')}>Handler URLs</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="In CI">
        <Code code={`abide check && abide build && bun test`} />

        <p><code>check</code> first, because a type error is the cheapest failure to report and the
        fastest to get. <code>build</code> next, because a bundle that will not build is the next
        cheapest, and the tests last, since they are the slowest to tell you anything.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/build-and-serve-the-app')}>Build &amp; start</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/build-and-serve-the-app')}>Build &amp; start</a> — the errors a
            build finds</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/abide-files')}><code>.abide</code> files</a> — the grammar
            being checked</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/cli')}>CLI</a> — every command and flag</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
